## Concept Overview
A closure is a fundamental JavaScript concept where an inner function maintains access to variables in its outer (lexical) scope, even when the outer function has completed execution. This creates a private scope for data encapsulation.

## Implementation Details
- Closures are created automatically when functions are defined inside other functions
- The inner function retains a reference to the outer function's variables
- This enables private variables and methods, similar to object-oriented encapsulation
- Memory is managed automatically - closures persist as long as references exist

## Code Example
```javascript
function createCounter() {
  let count = 0; // Private variable

  return {
    increment: function() {
      count++; // Accesses outer scope variable
      return count;
    },
    decrement: function() {
      count--;
      return count;
    },
    getCount: function() {
      return count;
    }
  };
}

const counter = createCounter();
console.log(counter.increment()); // 1
console.log(counter.increment()); // 2
console.log(counter.getCount());  // 2
```

## Common Pitfalls
- **Memory leaks**: Closures can prevent garbage collection if references persist unnecessarily
- **Loop confusion**: Variables in closures capture by reference, not value (use `let` or IIFE)
- **Performance impact**: Creating many closures can affect memory usage
- **`this` binding**: Arrow functions don't have their own `this`, which affects closure behavior

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to use closures for private state, manage asynchronous fetches with Promises, and implement simple caching and de-duplication without a framework.

## Key Concepts

- Closures for private state (cache and inFlight)
- Promises and in-flight request deduplication
- Time-based cache TTL (10s)
- Robust fetch error handling and URL composition

## Code Example

```javascript
function createLoader(baseUrl) {
  const cache = new Map();
  const inFlight = new Map();

  const join = (p) => {
    if (baseUrl.endsWith('/') || p.startsWith('/')) return `${baseUrl}${p}`;
    return `${baseUrl}/${p}`;
  };

  return {
    load(path) {
      const url = join(path);
      return fetch(url).then(r => {
        if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${r.status}`);
        return r.json();
      });
    },

    cachedLoad(path) {
      const key = path;
      const ttl = 10000; // 10 seconds
      const now = Date.now();
      const cached = cache.get(key);
      if (cached && now - cached.t < ttl) return Promise.resolve(cached.v);

      if (inFlight.has(key)) return inFlight.get(key);

      const url = join(path);
      const p = fetch(url).then(r => {
        if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${r.status}`);
        return r.json();
      }).then(v => {
        cache.set(key, { t: Date.now(), v });
        inFlight.delete(key);
        return v;
      }).catch(e => {
        inFlight.delete(key);
        throw e;
      });

      inFlight.set(key, p);
      return p;
    }
  };
}
```

